+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+- **Limited Condition Propagation:** Only the first layer of the decoder directly incorporates the condition; remaining layers operate only on internal feature representations, requiring the network to distinguish samples based on input alone [S2, unofficial].
+- **Re-execution Cost:** Requires re-execution of the feature extractor and decoder upon transfer function updates, although the compressed volume representation from the encoder stays constant [S1].
+- **Precision:** Standard floating-point concatenation is used; no special bit-packing is required beyond standard tensor operations.
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### HyperNetwork Conditioning
-Generates decoder weights from the latent code. Choose when the condition requires complex, non-linear modulation of the network's internal structure. [Link](12585-hypernetwork-conditioning.md)
+Generates decoder weights from the latent code. Choose when per-instance weight adaptation is needed. [Link](12585-hypernetwork-conditioning.md)
 
 ### Autoencoder/Autodecoder Conditioning
-Uses AdaIN to modulate intermediate feature tensors. Choose when the condition needs to influence the network at multiple depths, not just the input. [Link](12588-autoencoder-autodecoder-conditioning.md)
+Uses the decoder to reconstruct the input, often with a shared latent space. Choose for unsupervised or self-supervised learning. [Link](12588-autoencoder-autodecoder-conditioning.md)
 
 ### Global Conditioning
-Uses a separate global network or pooling to inject context. Choose when the condition is not a single vector but a more complex global representation. [Link](12589-global-conditioning.md)
+Uses global pooling or attention to inject conditions. Choose when spatial locality of conditions is important. [Link](12589-global-conditioning.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(D \cdot F_{in})$ for the concatenation step, where $D$ is latent dimension and $F_{in}$ is input feature size. The decoding complexity is unchanged from the base neural field.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(D)$ per instance to store the latent code. No additional memory for weight generation or modulation parameters.
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; computationally efficient at inference; allows pre-computation of the latent code if it is derived from static data [S1].
-- **Limitations:** The condition is only explicitly visible to the first layer, potentially limiting the network's ability to use complex conditional information [S2, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When the condition is a low-dimensional vector and the decoder is shallow or the condition is simple. When computational efficiency is prioritized over expressive power.
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(D + K)$ for concatenation; $O(N)$ for decoder evaluation, where $N$ is the number of parameters in the decoder.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(D)$ for storing the latent code per instance.
+- **Strengths:** Simple to implement; efficient inference as the encoder output is constant for a given instance [S1].
+- **Limitations:** The condition is only directly visible to the first layer, potentially limiting the decoder's ability to use the condition for complex transformations [S2, unofficial].
+- **When to Use:** When a simple, fast conditioning mechanism is sufficient and the decoder architecture allows the first layer to capture the necessary variance.
 
 ## Background
-Concatenation Conditioning is a standard technique in conditional generative modeling, adapted for neural fields in volume rendering. It was explicitly proposed for volumetric ambient occlusion prediction, where a latent vector encoding the volume is concatenated with Transfer Function descriptors to condition a generative decoder [S1]. It is also described as the "straightforward option" for implementing conditioning in neural fields, serving as a baseline for more sophisticated methods like HyperNetworks [S2, unofficial].
+Originated in the context of conditional generative modeling for volumetric data, specifically for predicting volumetric ambient occlusion [S1]. The method leverages the established practice of concatenating latent vectors with input descriptors in generative networks [S1].
 
